# dsh stack — the DeepSeek Harness, published at dsh.$TLD behind authentik.
#
# ./dsh/install.sh copies this to dsh/.env on first run and adds any key an
# older dsh/.env is missing (the TLD below comes from the root .env). dsh/.env
# is gitignored. ./setup.sh calls install.sh on every run, so this file is the
# one place to change how the harness is served.
#
# Unlike every other stack, dsh is NOT a container. `dsh web` refuses to bind
# anything but loopback and is remote code execution, so it runs as a host
# systemd *user* service and dsh-web-bridge.mjs forwards to it from the Docker
# gateway. See dsh/README.md.

# Only ever set in the root .env.example; the value below is expanded from it.
TLD=@TLD@

# The hostname the harness is served under: what the bridge passes to
# `dsh web --trusted-host` and what Traefik's router matches.
PUBLIC_HOST=dsh.@TLD@

# Where the bridge listens for Traefik. 172.17.0.1 is the host's Docker bridge
# gateway — the address host.docker.internal resolves to inside Traefik (which
# maps it with the `host-gateway` extra_host). Change it only if this host's
# Docker uses a different gateway.
LISTEN_HOST=172.17.0.1
LISTEN_PORT=3080

# The profile under $DSH_HOME/profiles to boot. `web` is the shipped web
# profile; dsh-mobile is installed into it.
DSH_PROFILE=web

# Stop the harness again after this many minutes with no browser connection,
# no session activity and no running tool. 0 keeps it up once started.
IDLE_MINUTES=20

# Version management. `latest` tracks the newest release. With
# DSH_REFRESH_ON_START=1 the bridge resolves and downloads that tag before
# every cold start, so each instance runs the newest release rather than
# whatever was fetched during the previous one. UPDATE_HOURS is how often it
# also checks in the background while running (0 disables that check).
DSH_UPDATE_TAG=latest
DSH_REFRESH_ON_START=1
UPDATE_HOURS=1

# The working directory a new session starts in. Left unset (or empty) the
# harness uses the service user's home — what `npx @deepseek-ai/dsh@latest web`
# gives you on a fresh machine. Set it to pin a workspace.
#DSH_CWD=/home/you/code

# Where ./dsh/install.sh clones dsh-mobile from. Point it at a fork or a local
# path if you carry your own copy.
DSH_MOBILE_REPO=https://github.com/notf0und/dsh-mobile.git
DSH_MOBILE_REF=main

# The directory-picker interaction. dsh chooses it once per boot: with a
# graphical session (DISPLAY/WAYLAND_DISPLAY) and zenity/kdialog on PATH it
# mounts its *native* chooser, which opens on this host's screen. Behind the
# bridge the browser is never here, so the bridge clears those two variables
# for the harness and dsh mounts its in-app browse picker — otherwise
# "add workspace" opens a dialog on the host that a remote browser cannot see
# and looks like nothing happened. Set this to 1 to keep the native chooser,
# for a browser running on this host's own desktop.
#DSH_NATIVE_PICKER=1

# On the first document it serves after a restart, the bridge sends
# `Clear-Site-Data: "cache"`. A browser can keep running the bundle it cached
# from the previous bridge (a plain refresh and a fresh PWA install both reuse
# that cache), so a change to the bridge or a patch it applies would not reach
# an existing profile without manually clearing site data on every device.
# This drops only the HTTP cache, once, and never storage. Set to 0 to disable.
#DSH_CACHE_CLEAR=0
